# Performance

Settings → Playing → **Performance** sets how fast the processor may run in your games. Left/Right (or A) change it; a change is
saved at once. The same choice is in [Quick settings](quick-settings.md#quick-settings) (MENU over a game). The Brick has no fan,
so there are no fan rows.

| Row | Choices |
|---|---|
| **Games: performance** | Battery, **Auto**, Max |

- **Auto** and **Max** both run a game at full speed on the Brick (its own "speeds up as needed" stayed too slow and the sound
  crackled). **Battery** holds it at one steady 1.4 GHz: enough for Game Boy and NES, and the battery lasts longer. After the game
  the Brick goes back to its own setting.

Values in bold are the defaults.

## Which setting a game uses

A game uses, in this order: what you saved for **this game** in the pause menu, what you saved for **its console**, then
**Games: performance** from this page. So the page sets the default for every game, and the pause menu (Options → Speed, rumble,
fan, then Save changes) makes exceptions for one console or one game. "Restore defaults" in the pause menu goes back to this
page's value. See [Options](options.md#speed-rumble-fan).

## Now

The bottom of the page shows, refreshed every second: the hottest temperature on the Brick, the clock of the processor and how
much power the battery delivers ("On the charger" while plugged in). It only reads; nothing on this part can be changed.
